Accession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Specimen Type  Breast  excisional tissue Procedure Date  REMOVED_DATE  Ordering Provider  CAPRICE CHRISTIAN GREENBERG M D  CASE  BS 09 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID PATIENT  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME Resident  Christian J  Davidson  M D  Pathologist  Jane E  Brock  M B   B S   Ph D  PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS  A  LEFT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR AN AREA OF ARCHITECTURAL DISTORTION  CARCINOMA IN SITU intermediate nuclear grade involving sclerosing adenosis  see Note  Columnar cell change with cysts  calcifications and atypia  Biopsy site changes  The margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis  B  RIGHT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A 4 CM AREA OF HETEROGENEOUS ENHANCEMENT  CARCINOMA IN SITU intermediate nuclear grade involving sclerosing adenosis  see Note  Columnar cell change with cysts  calcifications and atypia  Biopsy site changes  Fibroadenoma  0 4 cm  Apocrine cysts with calcifications  Intraductal papilloma  The margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis  Immunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the following results for CIS  block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Estrogen receptor POSITIVE Progesterone receptor POSITIVE REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID neu  c erb REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  NEGATIVE  0  NOTE  The CIS shows a solid pattern involving sclerosing adenosis with calcifications  which creates a morphological mimic of invasive carcinoma  several p63 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID double stains confirm retention of myoepithelial cells around involved ducts and lobules   Although the CIS lacks membranous E cadherin on stains performed  and in places is low nuclear grade typical of classic LCIS  many blocks show intermediate nuclear grade and increased mitoses which is not typical of classic LCIS  Both excisions lack necrosis although very rare single cell necrosis is seen  and the neoplastic cells are ER and PR positive  In part B  the right breast excision shows CIS with focally even greater nuclear pleomorphism and increased mitoses present but these areas still lack E cadherin membranous staining and are clearly part of the same neoplastic process  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   In both excisions  the CIS is present at or within 0 1 cm of all 6 margins  plus it is extensively present in 12 of 12 slides in both excisions  Overall  the lesions are very difficult to classify and predicting whether the biological behavior is more that of DCIS or LCIS is not possible  Some pathologists would certainly consider this to be pleomorphic LCIS  Dr  Susan Lester has reviewed selected slides from the case and concurs  CLINICAL DATA  History  None given  Operation  None given  Operative Findings  None given  Clinical Diagnosis  Bilateral breast masses  TISSUE SUBMITTED  A 1  Left breast loc   short superior  long lateral  B 2  Right breast loc  GROSS DESCRIPTION  The specimen is received fresh  in two parts  each labeled with the patient s name and unit number  RADIOLOGY  Mass and clip are in specimen  OR  Called  LBS  Clip in specimen  OR called  LBS  Part A  labeled   1  L breast wire localization short superior long lateral   consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue  oriented by sutures designating superior  short suture  and lateral  long suture  margins  The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins  posterior black  anterior blue  superior red  inferior green  lateral yellow  medial orange  inked by prosector   A localizing wire enters the superolateral aspect of the specimen and exits medially  Specimen is received with an accompanying radiograph demonstrating the localizing wire in place in the area of the central ill defined mass marked by a metallic surgical clip  Sectioning demonstrates a REMOVED_DATE cm hemorrhagic biopsy site filled with an amorphous material bearing the metallic clip  Tissue in this area is ill defined  firm and fibrous  maximally REMOVED_DATE cm   but a definitive mass or lesion cannot be identified  The irregular appearing fibrous tissue extends to the anterior  inferior  superior and medial margins  and approaches within REMOVED_DATE cm of the closest deep margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest lateral margin  The entire specimen is submitted  sequentially from medial to lateral  with perpendicular medial margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  perpendicular lateral margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and area of interest  per metallic clip  wire  in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Entire specimen  sequentially  multi frags  ESS  Please Note  Tissue is held for further fixation prior to processing  Part B  labeled   2  R breast wire loc long lateral short superior   consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue  oriented by sutures designating superior  short suture  and lateral  long suture  margins  The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins  posterior black  anterior blue  superior red  inferior green  lateral yellow  medial orange  inked by prosector   A localizing wire enters the superomedial aspect of the specimen and exits superolaterally  Specimen is received with an accompanying radiograph demonstrating a metallic clip in the lateral end of the specimen within an ill defined area of encircled abnormality  Please Note  Tissue in this area is focally surgically disrupted and is loosely associated by a black suture  tissue is partially reconstructed and likely true margins in this area have been inked  Sectioning in this area demonstrates a REMOVED_DATE cm hemorrhagic biopsy site filled with granular amorphous material bearing the metallic surgical clip  Surrounding tissue is fibrous but grossly unremarkable  without palpable or visible masses  lesions or other abnormalities grossly  Fibrous tissue is present at all inked margins and comprises approximately REMOVED_DATE   of the specimen  Tissue is held for further fixation prior to processing  Tissue is entirely submitted  sequentially from lateral to medial  with perpendicular lateral margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  perpendicular medial margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and area of interest per localizing wire  surgical clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Tissue from surgically disrupted area in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Entire specimen  sequentially from lateral to medial  multi frags  ESS  Immunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor  progesterone receptor  and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following criteria  Hormone receptor analysis  positive  10  of nuclei immunoreactive positive   low   0      10  of nuclei immunoreactive negative 0  of nuclei immunoreactive The lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet been determined  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID neu analysis  Negative  0   No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in  10  of tumor cells  Negative  1    Faint weak immunoreactivity in  10  of tumor cells but only a portion of the membrane is positive  Equivocal  2    Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in  10  of tumor cells  Positive  3    Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in  30  of tumor cells  Cytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result  External controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity  Some of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational use only  CASE NUMBER  REMOVED_CASE_ID DB ag By his her signature below  the senior physician certifies that he she personally conducted a microscopic examination   gross only  exam if so stated  of the described specimen s  and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis es  related thereto  Final Diagnosis by Jane E  Brock M B   B S   Ph D   Electronically sign